let's actually listen to now naomi long  of the alliance party who's uh now  addressing uh the press conference again  flash photography continues back to  scrutinizing ministers back to doing the  things that the assembly can do and yet  despite the fact that the vast majority  of people in northern ireland voted for  parties that wanted to return to  government and wanted to see the  assembly work and despite the fact that  even those who voted for the dup gave  them no mandate to block a return to the  assembly  we have found ourselves in that  situation today but whilst this is a sad  day for the people of northern ireland  it is a shameful day for the dup  today the dup came to stormont signed  the register  took their salaries but refused to take  their seats and do the work to earn it i  don't think that that is ever acceptable  but it is particularly unacceptable when  people in our constituencies are  struggling to feed their families  struggling to hate their homes worried  for their futures and it puts all of us  as politicians  in a place which is embarrassing yet  again where we want to serve the public  but are prevented from doing so  make no mistake the gulf between those  who got up today in the chamber and  bemoaned the cost of food in marx and  spencers and those who i represent who  get their food from food banks and it  just shows the detachment there is  between some people who come to this  chamber interested only in their party  self-interest and those of us who  actually come here to serve  i am appalled that we could not even  challenge the person who made this  decision because having been returned as  an mla only a week ago  he has disappeared off to his safety net  in parliament to the comfy green benches  rather than come here and do the job  that only a week ago he promised people  he would do  so this is a shameful day for the dup  but despite all of that we are here  ready to work  we may not be able to go into the  chamber and do the work as we intended  but we will be in our constituencies  working for the people who elected us i  will be in my ministerial office working  for the people who elected me and we  will continue to do all that we can  to make life better for the people of  northern ireland irrespective of those  who have nothing to offer but more drama  more division and more disruption it is  now time for them to call this to a  close allow us to get a speaker allow us  to move on and allow northern ireland to  move forward  thank you  naomi long there the alliance party also  saying that the people of northern  ireland in her opinion voted for parties  that wanted to see a an executive formed  uh following of course those comments we  just uh summarized from michelle o'neill  of sinn fein our senior island  correspondent david blevins  is is available to react with me now and  and david uh more of the same i guess uh  from both the alliance party but but  importantly as well from sinn fein  moments earlier  i think the word of the day is  frustration clearly that is what sinn  fein is expressing this was an historic  election for them the party overtaking  unionism for the very first time for the  for the first time in northern ireland's  history in fact there is an irish  nationalist first minister elect and  michelle o'neill is very keen to assume  that office and to get government up and  running again also deep frustration  there being expressed by naomi long her  party did exceptionally well in the  election they doubled their  representation in the stormwind assembly  indicating a shift to the middle ground  that more and more people are desiring a  different kind of politics here she's  also expressing her frustration that she  couldn't um challenge sir jeffrey  donaldson himself over this decision in  the house because of course he hasn't  taken his seat here yet he's remaining  as an mp at westminster until the issue  of the protocol is resolved but of  course for the dup it's a question of  frustration as well they've run out of  patience with the uk government over  that brexit border in the irish sea and  they say it's time for action not words  and until they get some action then they  say that they're not prepared to elect a  speaker and see the assembly functioning  or to become part of a power sharing  government so immense pressure now on  the prime minister he's got to make a  difficult decision  about how to resolve this and as you  heard from michelle o'neill she expects  boris johnson to be in northern ireland  next week along with the taoiseach the  irish prime minister michael martin  david blevins uh outside sermon thank  you
 

